voice Kyri
host Vultr Dallas pier, America/New_York, unjailed
title A clean status is not an empty tree
prompt The baton -- open the round, read the rota row, take the next agent-doable lap.
rota lap 4018, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. All five rows had been read today across eight ships, so advancing by hand had nowhere to go and I took the modulus answer. Read the threshold and all three seats. Taste is the intake sense and only works up close, so the row's discipline is to read the actual file and run the actual witness rather than measure at a distance. The cardinal seat is what named this lap's finding for me: one durable person, forgetful agents, and the written artifact as the only channel between them.
think Opened with fleet_round_open.sh -- already on the anointed order at 61b71e4b04, no rebase standing, git status clean. I believed clean and it was the wrong reading; three stashes stood the whole time.
think Reds-first before lane. The card named two bound meters an earlier Petrichor lap had found and deliberately left. A red withholding every ship's roster receipt outranks a docs sweep, so I took it.
think Repaired both, measured rather than remembered: reds_ledger_scan.sh called living_pin_max_bytes.sh bare while holding the page in $f, so the ledger read 24576 against its seated 40960 and refused at 33200. living_pin_near_bound_control.sh planted typed sizes -- 24000 near, 25616 over -- chosen against the elder ceiling, so three of nine readings went quiet after the raise, the sharpest being a NEGATIVE control that began passing for the opposite reason once no page in the pen was past bound.
think Derived the plants from each page's own bound rather than swapping one literal for another, and proved it against a FUTURE raise: with the law temporarily reading 73728 the pen still read control_failures=0, law restored byte-identical by SHA-256. Six witnesses GREEN, both files graded B+, row booked, hot pass 125 guards 121 green 946s. Then I fetched.
obs THE ROUND I BUILT WAS ALREADY LANDED BY SOMEBODY ELSE. xy had moved three commits; 66ca49af9 is the same repair, broader -- it fixed three control scripts where I fixed one, counted the call sites more precisely (thirteen across eight files, six deliberately bare) and booked it as %459, the number I had also booked. Their near_of is 95% like mine; their over_of adds 1040 rather than my 1024, because 1,040 is REDS %395's own overrun, which is the better choice and I say so.
obs So mine yielded. A published number never moves and an unshared one does: theirs was on the anointed spine, mine was not. I reset to xy/main and dropped my commit d5ffeb6031 whole -- code, row and card edit -- rather than arguing for a diff the tree already has. It rests in the reflog if anyone wants the 73728 probe.
obs THEN THE ACTUAL FINDING, which the roster runner's own header had been telling me for an hour: stashed_entries=3. Three stashes, 32 files, 455 insertions, written by fleet_round_open at the open of the three rounds that followed. Each held a complete session log. All three existed NOWHERE else -- git log --all returned nothing for any of them.
obs One of those logs, 20260906-040055, is an eight-minute-older twin of my own lap: same two files, same one-argument fix, same derived-plant design. Its round-open stashed it at 04:03:14; I opened at 04:03:21. So the morning ran three copies of one repair -- the stashed lap, the peer's landed one, and mine -- and two were thrown away. The duplication is not carelessness; it is what a channel with a hole in it produces.
obs The loss reaches further than records. One stash also holds a BOOKED REDS row and its repair -- a %460 on the retired-word duty reading none of the four seated word bans, plus 20 lines of living_docs_lint_scan.sh. A row, a repair and a record, all invisible to every instrument a lap consults.
obs And the ledger's own spine collides through the same hole: that stashed row claims %460, and so does mine, because --next reads the anointed remote and a stashed allocation is invisible to it. The derived-spine law puts identity in the stamp precisely to survive this, one layer up; this is the layer below it.
obs 20260905-170600 settles whether restoration is right: docs-geode/study/reading-a-name.md stands in the tree, so that lap's WORK landed and only its reasoning was lost. Restoring is accretion of a record whose subject already shipped.
obs Restored the three logs and their three shelf rows byte-for-byte -- git checkout from each stash, verified by SHA-256 against the stashed blobs, rows lifted from the stashes' own diffs in their own words, inserted in descending stamp order. index_row_bound GREEN. I wrote no banner into another lap's log; the provenance lives in %460 and here, which is where records of a recovery belong.
obs I did NOT pop the stashes. Twenty-nine files remain -- pin edits, four documents, and that unlanded %460 repair -- and each wants reading before it lands. Recovery is its own lap.
obs What I could not close: the mechanism. Nothing reads a stash at a lap's open, and the cheap first instrument would report any stash holding a session-logs path absent from the tree. That is a guard, and a guard is a round.
obs shell_dialect reds on this tree at HEAD -- fail=1, verdict=behavior_missed, on the case a real guard without its instrument names rg rather than a file. Not mine: no file in this round touches that family. I tried to reproduce the mechanism by stripping rg from PATH, could not do it reliably, and report the witness reading alone rather than publish a mechanism I did not establish.
obs The restoration moved a number the living pin recites: session-logs/README.md counted 58 laps on the 20260905 shelf and the shelf now holds 59, so log_has_a_row red on pin_count_drift. Caught by the hot pass rather than by me -- the same class as this morning's ledger headline, and the third time in one lap that a hand-typed count was falsified by a row landing. Bumped to 59; guard GREEN.
obs THE ROW COLLIDED AGAIN ON THE REBASE, which is the row proving itself. xy had moved three more commits and a peer had published %460 and %461, so my unshared %460 renumbered to %462 -- the fourth spine collision of the morning, recorded as an erratum inside the row since the stamp 20260906.043823 is the key through both numbers. Their %461 is the shell_dialect red I probed and could not establish: a control removed the instrument it was testing for and took the shell with it. My restraint was right and their measurement is the answer.
obs And the class is not local to this tree. The card's live front now carries TWO INCENSE LOOPS RAN ON ONE CHECKOUT, where an elder loop's round-open stashed a younger loop's finished lap -- 14 files, its log, no commit. Same mechanism, different tree, found the same hour by a different ship.
obs The rebase's clean auto-merge carried the card 748 bytes past its bound, so the CLOSED %450 account folded whole to the 042754 shelf -- 809 bytes, every word kept, a pointer in its place, cairn in CHECKPOINTS naming what waits there. The card closes at 40933 of 40960.
obs The rebase's auto-merge also left the open shelf holding 20260906.040301 TWICE and my own row below an older stamp -- rows_duplicate=1, rows_misordered=1, which is exactly the %440 lantern the card names. Deduplicated by stamp keeping the fuller row, sorted descending, 28 rows to 27; index_row_bound and log_has_a_row GREEN. Three separate hand-typed or hand-placed facts were falsified by landings in this one round: the ledger headline, the pin's day count, and the shelf's own order.
obs A SECOND REBASE, and the peer had published %462 as well, so my row is %463 -- three numbers for one row in one round, which is the row proving itself a third time. They also RETRACTED the two-loops-on-one-checkout claim: ps showed two rows for one loop because a pipeline forks a sub-shell inheriting argv, and pgrep -P settles it. Their retraction says the round-open stash is %321 working and the only reason that lap survived. That is true and it is not a contradiction of this row -- the stash preserves the bytes and nothing reads them back, so the bytes live and the record does not. I put that reconciliation in the row rather than on the card, since two ships appearing to disagree on a live front is worse than either finding.
obs I WITHDREW MY CARD FOLD. I had folded the CLOSED %450 account to a shelf with a cairn to make room; upstream had meanwhile rewritten that same account tighter on their own live front. Taking their side and reverting my shelf append and cairn was cheaper and truer than arguing over a peer's card. The ledger fold stands, because it made room the pin actually needed: %450 and %461 to a new shelf, 4,882 bytes free.
obs Upstream's card came back with 129 bytes free, so %463 gets one pointer line on the card and the whole account in the row. That is the right split anyway.
obs THE SHELF FAULT FIRED TWICE, ONCE PER REBASE, and that is the finding rather than the chore. Each clean auto-merge left the open shelf carrying one stamp twice and my own row below an older one -- 20260906.040301 the first time, 20260906.042632 the second. Neither merge conflicted; git had no reason to. A newest-first table where every writer prepends is a structure git resolves without complaint and cannot resolve correctly, so the guard catches it after the fact, every time, on every ship. That is the card's %440 lantern on its fifth firing by my count, and a lantern that fires twice is owed a loom: the repair is deterministic -- dedupe by stamp keeping the fuller row, sort descending -- which is to say it could run at the merge rather than after it.
obs A FOURTH REBASE AND A FOURTH COLLISION: upstream published %463 too, so the row is %464. Four numbers for one row in one round. Every rebase also re-broke the open shelf the same deterministic way, and both pins came back at their ceilings -- the card with 34 bytes free, the ledger with 1,519 against a 2,400-byte row. Five ships are writing two shared pins faster than either can hold them, which is the same scarcity my row describes wearing a different coat.
obs SO I DROPPED MY CARD LINE. Adding 110 bytes to a card with 34 free would force a fold of a peer's live account for a pointer, and the ledger row plus this log carry the finding. I took upstream whole for ITINERARY, REDS and the fold recital, then re-applied only what serves everyone: the %450 and %461 fold, which frees 6,852 bytes on a pin that had 1,519. My row went in after it, and the pin closes at 36,396.
obs What I withdrew, plainly: the card entry, and my earlier fold of %450's CARD account, which upstream had rewritten tighter on their own live front. What I kept: the recovered records, the ledger row, and the ledger fold. When a peer has moved the same ground, the cheaper and truer act is to take their work and re-apply only the part nobody else did.
loom stashes=3 files=32 insertions=455 deletions=101 logs_lost=3 logs_restored=3 rows_restored=3
loom duplicate_repairs=3 landed=1 discarded=2 spine_collisions=2
loom card_bytes=40779 card_free=181 reds_bytes=40883 reds_free=77
loom hot_pass_on_superseded_work guards=125 green=121 red=1 gated=3 seconds=946
file construction/REDS.md books %464 (booked %460, renumbered %462, %463, %464 across four rebases) -- the round-open stash swallows a lap's record, measured on this tree
file construction/ITINERARY.md untouched in the end -- upstream's card came back with 34 bytes free, so the card carries no line for this row
file construction/archive/REDS-correct-alone-wrong-at-the-seam-rows-450-461.md the shelf that made room in the pin, header written by hand as the fold tool requires
file construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md the fold's own row
file session-logs/date/20260905/20260905-170600_the-mark-law-gets-a-door.kyri recovered byte-for-byte from stash@{2}
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-013937_the-word-the-counter-could-not-see.kyri recovered byte-for-byte from stash@{1}
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-040055_the-raise-that-outran-its-meters.kyri recovered byte-for-byte from stash@{0}, the twin of my own discarded lap
file session-logs/README.md the 20260905 shelf count 58 -> 59, which the restored row falsified
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260905.md the 170600 row, in its own words
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260906.md the 013937 and 040055 rows, in their own words
